{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FwJzvyeypMEkJ7RsfPvTT3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good, the directory exists. Now let me write the complete LaTeX paper. I need to be careful to:\n\n1. Include all experiments from the JSON\n2. Base all numbers on the actual data\n3. Structure as: Abstract, Introduction, Related Work, Method, Experiments, Conclusion\n4. Follow the narrative guidance and paper framing\n\nLet me organize the experiments:\n\n1. Multi-seed stability verification\n2. CosineSim baseline diagnostic\n3. Full-data training (conf≥1)\n4. LF ensemble oracle baseline\n5. LLM-as-judge baseline\n6. Mean-pooling + LR baseline\n\n\n7. Error analysis\n8. Cross-agent transfer (OpenHands)\n9. Temporal label model\n10. FlyingSquid baseline\n11. Outcome BP sensitivity analysis\n12. Window size ablation\n13. Architecture ablation\n14. LF hold-one-out ablation\n15. SupCon auxiliary loss ablation\n16. Non-NLP structural baseline\n17. Encoder ablation\n18. Human annotation gold set\n19. LF temporal dynamics analysis\n20. Data scaling curve\n21. Early termination simulation\n22. Interpretability visualization\n\nNow I'm mapping out the paper structure with Abstract, Introduction to motivate the three core claims, Related Work covering data programming and agent monitoring, then Methods, Experiments, and Conclusion sections.\n\nFor Methods, I'll cover the problem formulation for window-level progress estimation, describe the four labeling functions, analyze their temporal dynamics, introduce the temporal label model with FlyingSquid as a baseline, and detail the ProgressProbe architecture using GRU with attention. The Experiments section will start with setup details including the human gold set and data statistics, present main results comparing all methods, contrast the label model approaches, show that the student exceeds the teacher with oracle baselines, compare LLM-as-Judge performance, and demonstrate cross-agent transfer capabilities.\n\nThen I'm running through ablations on window size, architecture choices, encoder selection, labeling function hold-one-out analysis, and data scaling effects. I'm also testing non-NLP structural baselines, mean-pooling, and cosine similarity approaches, plus robustness checks across multiple seeds, full-data training with noise, and outcome backpropagation sensitivity. There's error analysis, interpretability studies, and an application to early termination, followed by discussion, limitations, and conclusions.\n\nThat's a lot of experiments to fit into a paper. I need to organize this strategically—putting the core results in the main body and moving the ablations to the appendix. So I'll feature the gold set comparison across all methods, the label model progression from majority voting through FlyingSquid to TLM, temporal role analysis of the labeling functions, cross-agent transfer results, and LLM-as-judge comparisons as my main tables. The appendix will hold the ablations on window size, architecture, encoder, hold-one-out analysis, data scaling, multi-seed stability, outcome sensitivity, structural baselines, early termination, and interpretability findings. Now I'm ready to start writing.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":347,"cache_read_input_tokens":42130,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":347},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c62cb88a-9d2d-484c-a1fc-f28f765eb6cd","uuid":"437c4063-b268-4cf1-a5c4-8a6c1ef48908"}
